Jul 17 11:23:41 srv-ad.entrepriseY.local Microsoft-Windows-Security-Auditing[652]: An account was successfully logged on.    Subject:  #011Security ID:#011#011S-1-0-0  #011Account Name:#011#011-  #011Account Domain:#011#011-  #011Logon ID:#011#0110x0    Logon Information:  #011Logon Type:#011#0113  #011Restricted Admin Mode:#011-  #011Virtual Account:#011#011No  #011Elevated Token:#011#011Yes    Impersonation Level:#011#011Impersonation    New Logon:  #011Security ID:#011#011S-1-5-18  #011Account Name:#011#011SRV-AD$  #011Account Domain:#011#011ENTREPRISEY.LOCAL  #011Logon ID:#011#0110x1126483  #011Linked Logon ID:#011#0110x0  #011Network Account Name:#011-  #011Network Account Domain:#011-  #011Logon GUID:#011#011{bccc18c7-0758-8ec3-bcd9-ea28df712fb0}    Process Information:  #011Process ID:#011#0110x0  #011Process Name:#011#011-    Network Information:  #011Workstation Name:#011-  #011Source Network Address:#011::1  #011Source Port:#011#01160934    Detailed Authentication Information:  #011Logon Process:#011#011Kerberos  #011Authentication Package:#011Kerberos  #011Transited Services:#011-  #011Package Name (NTLM only):#011-  #011Key Length:#011#0110    This event is generated when a logon session is created. It is generated on the computer that was accessed.    The subject fields indicate the account on the local system which requested the logon. This is most commonly a service such as the Server service, or a local process such as Winlogon.exe or Services.exe.    The logon type field indicates the kind of logon that occurred. The most common types are 2 (interactive) and 3 (network).    The New Logon fields indicate the account for whom the new logon was created, i.e. the account that was logged on.    The network fields indicate where a remote logon request originated. Workstation name is not always available and may be left blank in some cases.    The impersonation level field indicates the extent to which a process in the logon session can impersonate.    The authentication information fields provide detailed information about this specific logon request.  #011- Logon GUID is a unique identifier that can be used to correlate this event with a KDC event.  #011- Transited services indicate which intermediate services have participated in this logon request.  #011- Package name indicates which sub-protocol was used among the NTLM protocols.  #011- Key length indicates the length of the generated session key. This will be 0 if no session key was requested.#015
